- The validation process must confirm the validity of a qualified electronic signature only if all of the following were true at the time of signing:  
  - the supporting certificate was a qualified certificate for electronic signature complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a));  
  - the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid (E Art. 32.1(b));  
  - the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
  - the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
  - any use of a pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used (E Art. 32.1(e));  
  - the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f));  
  - the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g)); and  
  - the requirements provided for in Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)).

- The validation system must provide the relying party with the correct result of the validation process and allow the relying party to detect any security-relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).

- The Commission may adopt implementing acts establishing reference standards for validation; compliance with Article 32(1) is presumed where validation meets those standards (E Art. 32.3).

- A qualified validation service may be provided only by a qualified trust service provider that (a) provides validation in compliance with Article 32(1), and (b) allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated manner which is reliable and efficient (E Art. 33.1). (The remainder of paragraph (b) is not present in the provided context.)

- Verification must be done by appropriate means and in accordance with national law, either directly by the qualified trust service provider or by relying on a third party (E Art. 24.1).

- Permitted verification methods (E Art. 24.1):
  - (a) by the physical presence of the natural person or of an authorised representative of the legal person (E Art. 24.1(a));
  - (b) remotely, using electronic identification means, provided that prior to issuance a physical presence was ensured and the e‑ID meets the assurance levels "substantial" or "high" set out in Article 8 (E Art. 24.1(b));
  - (c) by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or of a qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with point (a) or (b) (E Art. 24.1(c));
  - (d) by other identification methods recognised at national level that provide equivalent assurance in terms of reliability to physical presence (E Art. 24.1(d)).

- If method (d) is used, the equivalent assurance must be confirmed by a conformity assessment body (E Art. 24).

- Choose a processor who provides “sufficient guarantees” to implement appropriate technical and organisational measures so that processing meets the Regulation’s requirements and protects data‑subjects’ rights (G Art. 28.1; G Rec. 81).

- Put the processing relationship in writing: processing by a processor must be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law, binding the processor to the controller, and specifying the subject‑matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purpose of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects, and the obligations and rights of the controller (G Art. 28.3).

- Ensure the contract contains specific obligations, in particular that the processor:
  - processes personal data only on documented instructions from the controller (including for transfers), unless required by law (G Art. 28.3.a);
  - at the controller’s choice deletes or returns all personal data after provision of services and deletes existing copies unless law requires storage (G Art. 28.3.g);
  - makes available to the controller all information necessary to demonstrate compliance and allows/contributes to audits and inspections (G Art. 28.3.h);
  - immediately informs the controller if an instruction infringes the Regulation or other Union/Member State data protection law (G Art. 28.3.h).

- Regulate sub‑processors: the processor must not engage another processor without the controller’s prior specific or general written authorisation; with general authorisation the processor must inform the controller of intended additions/replacements so the controller can object (G Art. 28.2). Where a processor engages another processor, the same data protection obligations must be imposed on that other processor (G Art. 28.4).

- Use adherence to an approved code of conduct or an approved certification mechanism as evidence of sufficient guarantees where appropriate (G Rec. 81).

- Who must keep a record
  - Each controller (and, where applicable, the controller's representative) shall maintain a record of processing activities under its responsibility (G Art. 30.1).  
  - Each processor (and, where applicable, the processor's representative) shall maintain a record of all categories of processing activities carried out on behalf of a controller (G Art. 30.2).

- Minimum contents for a controller's record (G Art. 30.1)
  - (a) name and contact details of the controller and, where applicable, the joint controller, the controller's representative and the data protection officer (G Art. 30.1.a);  
  - (b) the purposes of the processing (G Art. 30.1.b);  
  - (c) a description of the categories of data subjects and of the categories of personal data (G Art. 30.1.c);  
  - (d) the categories of recipients to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, including recipients in third countries or international organisations (G Art. 30.1.d);  
  - (e) where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation (G Art. 30.1.e);  
  - (f) where possible, the envisaged time limits for erasure of the different categories of data (G Art. 30.1.f);  
  - (g) where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (G Art. 30.1.g).

- Minimum contents for a processor's record (G Art. 30.2)
  - (a) the name and contact details of the processor or processors and of each controller on behalf of which the processor is acting, and, where applicable, of the controller's or the processor's representative, and the data protection officer (G Art. 30.2.a);  
  - (b) the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller (G Art. 30.2.b);  
  - (c) where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation (G Art. 30.2.c);  
  - (d) where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (G Art. 30.2.d).

- Access and cooperation with supervisory authorities
  - The controller or the processor and, where applicable, their representative shall make the record available to the supervisory authority on request (G Art. 30.4).  
  - Controllers and processors should be obliged to cooperate with the supervisory authority and make those records, on request, available to it (G Rec. 82).

- Purpose and scope: codes are intended to contribute to the proper application of the Regulation, taking account of sector‑specific features and the needs of micro, small and medium‑sized enterprises (Art. 40.1).

- Specific items that a code may specify (Art. 40.2):
  - fair and transparent processing (Art. 40.2(a));
  - the legitimate interests pursued by controllers in specific contexts (Art. 40.2(b));
  - the collection of personal data (Art. 40.2(c));
  - the pseudonymisation of personal data (Art. 40.2(d));
  - the information provided to the public and to data subjects (Art. 40.2(e));
  - the exercise of the rights of data subjects (Art. 40.2(f));
  - the information provided to, and the protection of, children, and how parental responsibility consent is to be obtained (Art. 40.2(g));
  - the measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and the measures to ensure security of processing referred to in Article 32 (Art. 40.2(h));
  - the notification of personal data breaches to supervisory authorities and the communication of such breaches to data subjects (Art. 40.2(i));
  - the transfer of personal data to third countries or international organisations (Art. 40.2(j));
  - out‑of‑court proceedings and other dispute resolution procedures for resolving disputes between controllers and data subjects with regard to processing (without prejudice to rights under Articles 77 and 79) (Art. 40.2(k)).

- Procedural element: when drawing up, amending or extending a code, associations and other bodies should consult relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible (Rec. 99).

- Calibration guidance: codes may calibrate controllers’ and processors’ obligations taking account of the risk to rights and freedoms of natural persons (Rec. 98).

- General rule: where a non-contractual obligation “concerns a relationship existing between the parties” (e.g. arising out of a contract or a tort/delict) and is closely connected with that relationship, it is governed by the law that governs that relationship (RII Art. 11.1).

- If the law applicable cannot be determined under Art. 11.1:
  - and the parties have their habitual residence in the same country when the event giving rise to the damage occurs, the law of that country applies (RII Art. 11.2);
  - if not determined by Art. 11.1 or 11.2, the law of the country in which the act was performed applies (RII Art. 11.3);
  - if, from all the circumstances, the obligation is manifestly more closely connected with a different country, the law of that other country applies (RII Art. 11.4).

- Special rule for dealings prior to conclusion of a contract (culpa in contrahendo): the applicable law is the law that applies to the contract or that would have applied had the contract been concluded (RII Art. 12.1). If that cannot be determined, the fallback rules are (a) the law of the country in which the damage occurs, (b) where the parties share habitual residence at the relevant time, the law of that country, or (c) if manifestly more closely connected with another country, the law of that other country (RII Art. 12.2(a)–(c)).
